# Repro for -ATTEDIT Position (interactive command).
#
# -ATTEDIT is interactive, so it is reproduced by typing at the command line (the JSON `run` form
# never opens prompts). Set up a block with one attribute, then drive -ATTEDIT one at a time and
# choose Position.
#
# Setup (non-interactive). The attdef handle is the last entity in a fresh sample; capture it, then
# make the block and insert it (handles are hex strings):
attdef {"tag":"TAG","at":[10,0],"default":"D","height":2.5}
#   block  {"name":"B","base":[0,0],"handles":["<attdef-handle>"],"keep":"delete"}
#   insert {"name":"B","at":[0,0],"attribs":{"TAG":"hello"}}
#
# Then at the command line (the behaviour under test):
#   -ATTEDIT
#   Edit attributes one at a time? <Y>
#   Enter block name specification <Enter>
#   Enter attribute tag specification <Enter>
#   Enter attribute value specification <Enter>
#   Select attributes: all<Enter><Enter>
#   Enter an option: Position<Enter>
#   Enter new insertion point for attribute <TAG>: 50,60<Enter>
#   Enter an option: <Enter>       (Next -> done)
#
# Before the fix: Position answers "Position is not available yet."
# After the fix: the attribute's insertion point moves to the picked point.
#
# The engine test dash_attedit_position_moves_the_attribute drives this and asserts the move.
